/* crosswalk cards, a-bio. */
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["5·bio"]=[{"q":"A cross of a pure smooth RR and a pure wrinkled rr gives Rr. What is the Rr plant called?","a":"a hybrid","w":["a gamete","a recessive","a test cross"],"why":"A hybrid carries one copy of each."},
 {"q":"From Rr × Rr, RR, Rr and rr each have chance 1/3. True?","a":"no: 1/4, 1/2, 1/4","w":["yes: three genotypes","yes: by equally likely cases","no: 1/2, 1/4, 1/4"],"why":"The equally likely cases are the four pairs; Rr is two of them."},
 {"q":"3 : 1, so of these four peas three are smooth. True?","a":"no: the ratio is of chances","w":["yes: by the grid","yes: by the expected count","no: two of them are smooth"],"why":"Each pea is its own stage; four peas may all be wrinkled."},
 {"q":"Smooth is dominant, so most peas are smooth. True?","a":"no: dominance says what a hybrid shows","w":["yes: dominant forms spread","yes: by the 3 : 1 count","no: most are wrinkled"],"why":"It says nothing of how common either allele is."},
 {"q":"What a plant shows, smooth or wrinkled. What does the page call it?","a":"its look","w":["its genotype","its allele","its gamete"],"why":"The genotype is the pair of copies it carries."},
 {"q":"A plant is crossed with rr to read what it carries. What is the cross called?","a":"a test cross","w":["a hybrid cross","a self cross","a pure cross"],"why":"An rr parent hands on only r, so the other parent shows."},
 {"q":"Six smooth seeds from a test cross, so the parent is RR. True?","a":"no: likely RR, never proved","w":["yes: an Rr parent shows wrinkled","yes: by the expected count","no: it is Rr"],"why":"An Rr parent gives six smooth in 1 of 64 lists."},
 {"q":"From Rr Yy × Rr Yy, the chance of smooth or yellow as 3/4 + 3/4. What is wrong?","a":"it is more than 1","w":["it should be 3/4 · 3/4","it should be 9/16","nothing: chances add"],"why":"Count the cells: only wrinkled green fails, so 15 of 16."},
 {"q":"R and r, two forms of one gene. What is each called?","a":"an allele","w":["a gamete","a genotype","a hybrid"],"why":"A genotype is a pair of alleles."},
 {"q":"A plant is Rr Yy. How many kinds of gamete does it make?","a":"4","w":["2","16","9"],"why":"One allele of each gene: {R, r} × {Y, y}."},
 {"q":"Rr Yy × Rr Yy. What share of the seeds is wrinkled and green?","a":"1/16","w":["1/4","3/16","9/16"],"why":"Wrinkled 1/4 times green 1/4, the last of 9 : 3 : 3 : 1."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["9·bio"]=[{"q":"B ≈ 70 M^(3/4) kcal/day. What is the 3/4 called?","a":"the exponent of the power law","w":["its constant a","the mass, in kg","the rate per kilogram"],"why":"f(M) = a · M^b: b is the exponent, the scaling exponent."},
 {"q":"A power law with b = 3/4. The mass is multiplied by 16. What is the rate multiplied by?","a":"8","w":["12","16","4"],"why":"Power-law habit: the factor is 16^(3/4) = 2³."},
 {"q":"“u(kM)^b = k · uM^b.” What is wrong?","a":"the factor comes out as kᵇ, not k","w":["nothing: the k comes out","the u should be raised to b","M cannot be multiplied"],"why":"Power-law habit: f(kM) = kᵇ f(M). Only for b = 1 is it k."},
 {"q":"Which exponent makes doubling the mass double the output?","a":"b = 1","w":["b = 3/4","b = 2/3","b = 2"],"why":"Only b = 1, direct proportion, gives 2ᵇ = 2."},
 {"q":"f(M) = 5M^(1/2). What is f(9M)/f(M)?","a":"3","w":["9","4.5","45"],"why":"The constant drops out: 9^(1/2) = 3."},
 {"q":"B/M = 70 M^(−1/4). As the mass grows, what does the rate per kilogram do?","a":"falls, and never reaches 0","w":["rises without bound","stays the same","falls to 0 at last"],"why":"A power law with a negative exponent, read as 9e read 1/x and the powers."},
 {"q":"What is a kilocalorie?","a":"4184 J","w":["1000 J","4.184 J","86 400 J"],"why":"The dietary calorie: about what warms 1 kg of water by 1 °C."},
 {"q":"Mass ratio K and rate ratio L, both above 1, with K² < L³. What follows for b?","a":"b > 2/3","w":["b < 2/3","b = 2/3","b > 3/2"],"why":"Bracket-an-exponent: K^(2/3) < L = Kᵇ, and power-grows."},
 {"q":"Is a power law a technique?","a":"no: it is a rule found by measuring","w":["yes: power-law habit","yes, when b is rational","no: it is a definition"],"why":"Being measured, it is a law of nature; the techniques are about the function it fits."},
 {"q":"Mass ratio a million. How do the ⅔ law and the ¾ law compare?","a":"¾ predicts about √10 times more","w":["⅔ predicts about √10 times more","they predict the same","¾ predicts 10 times more"],"why":"10⁶^(2/3) = 10⁴, and 10⁶^(3/4) = 10⁴ · √10."},
 {"q":"Mouse to rat is ten times the mass; person to cow is too. Which cuts the rate per kilogram more?","a":"neither: both cut it by 10^(1/4)","w":["mouse to rat","person to cow","it depends on the constant"],"why":"Power-law habit: only the ratio of masses matters."},
 {"q":"Measurements that scale with an animal’s size by power laws. What is their study called?","a":"allometry","w":["metabolism","basal rate","the surface law"],"why":"Allometry, with b the scaling exponent."},
 {"q":"A creature’s resting energy use grows as its mass to the power 3/4. What is it called?","a":"the three-quarters law (Kleiber's)","w":["the diagonal argument (Cantor's)","the chain estimate (Fermi's)","the dot-bound technique (Cauchy's and Schwarz's)"],"why":"The page names it the three-quarters law, and gives the accepted name in brackets: Kleiber's."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10·bio"]=[{"q":"Invertase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose. What is sucrose to invertase?","a":"its substrate","w":["its product","its turnover number","its ceiling"],"why":"The substance an enzyme acts on is its substrate."},
 {"q":"An enzyme speeds up one change and is not used up. Is it a substrate?","a":"no: it is a catalyst, an enzyme","w":["yes: it is changed","yes: it is in the solution","no: it is a product"],"why":"Catalysis speeds a change; the enzyme comes out as it went in."},
 {"q":"v(s) = V_max · s/(K_m + s). What is lim_{s→∞} v(s)?","a":"V_max, the ceiling","w":["K_m","V_max/K_m","none: v grows past every bound"],"why":"Both degrees are 1; the leading coefficients give V_max/1."},
 {"q":"Is there an s ≥ 0 with v(s) = V_max?","a":"no: never-reached","w":["yes, far enough out","yes, at s = K_m","yes, at s = 99 K_m"],"why":"V_max − v(s) = V_max K_m/(K_m + s), a positive over a positive."},
 {"q":"At s = K_m, what is v?","a":"half the ceiling, ½ V_max","w":["the ceiling, V_max","the largest rate","V_max/K_m"],"why":"Half-way: V_max K_m/(2K_m) = ½ V_max."},
 {"q":"K_m = 2 mmol/L and s = 18 mmol/L. What share of the ceiling is v?","a":"90%","w":["10%","50%","100%"],"why":"s/(K_m + s) = 18/20."},
 {"q":"Substrate scarce, s far below K_m. How does v behave?","a":"nearly proportional to s","w":["nearly at the ceiling","nearly constant","it halves as s doubles"],"why":"Low-end: v(s)/s heads to V_max/K_m as s → 0⁺."},
 {"q":"Glucose at 5 mmol/L meets an enzyme with K_m = 0.05 mmol/L. What is the enzyme?","a":"saturated: near its ceiling","w":["half busy","nearly proportional to supply","at exactly its ceiling"],"why":"s is a hundred times K_m, so v is about 99% of V_max."},
 {"q":"1/v = (K_m/V_max) · (1/s) + 1/V_max. What is 1/V_max in it?","a":"the start of the linear function in 1/s","w":["its step","the share at K_m","where it is 0"],"why":"At u = 1/s = 0 the line gives 1/V_max: the ceiling’s reciprocal."},
 {"q":"1/(K_m + s) = 1/K_m + 1/s. Is the move legal?","a":"no: a sum is not flipped term by term","w":["yes: flip each term","yes, for s > 0","no: K_m must be 1"],"why":"At K_m = s = 1: ½ against 2."},
 {"q":"Twice the enzyme, same substrate. What changes?","a":"V_max doubles; K_m stays","w":["K_m doubles; V_max stays","both double","neither changes"],"why":"V_max = k_cat · E_total; K_m is a property of the pair."},
 {"q":"Only that the busy enzyme holds steady, not that grab and let-go balance. What follows?","a":"the same function, K_m a mix of rates","w":["a different function of s","no ceiling","a linear function of s"],"why":"The 1925 steady-state road reaches the same v(s)."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["14·bio"]=[{"q":"x² = 2Dt. What is D called?","a":"the diffusion coefficient","w":["the demand","the distance","the oxygen supply"],"why":"A constant for the molecule and the liquid."},
 {"q":"Oxygen spreads twice as far. How much longer does it take?","a":"four times as long","w":["twice as long","√2 times as long","the same time"],"why":"t = x²/(2D) grows with x²."},
 {"q":"A sheet of tissue, bathed on both faces. Where is the oxygen least?","a":"at the middle","w":["at the faces","spread evenly","at one face"],"why":"The curve inside the sheet has its bottom at the middle."},
 {"q":"L_max = √(2Dc₀/q). What does it mark?","a":"the half-thickness where the middle just runs out","w":["the whole thickness of the sheet","the time to spread","the oxygen at the faces"],"why":"c at the middle is 0 there."},
 {"q":"q doubles. What happens to L_max?","a":"it shrinks by √2","w":["it halves","it doubles","it stays"],"why":"L_max goes as 1/√q."},
 {"q":"c(0) < 0 for a sheet too thick. What failed?","a":"the premise: a part with no oxygen cannot use it at rate q","w":["the formula’s algebra","D is wrong","nothing: the core holds negative oxygen"],"why":"The premise fails before the number goes below 0."},
 {"q":"The time from a face to the middle of the thickest sheet. What is it?","a":"c₀/q","w":["L_max/D","2D/L_max","q/c₀"],"why":"Put L_max into t = x²/(2D)."},
 {"q":"“Average speed 2D/x for every x, however small.” What is wrong?","a":"the premise holds only over many collisions","w":["nothing","D is not constant","x² = 2Dt is exact"],"why":"At tiny x it beats any real molecule’s speed."},
 {"q":"Blood carries oxygen 1 m at 0.1 m/s. Diffusion would take years. What does that show?","a":"a large body needs a flow, not diffusion alone","w":["diffusion is faster","blood is too slow","the premise fails"],"why":"Spreading time grows with the square of the distance."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["17·bio"]=[{"q":"P′ = rP. Solution?","a":"P₀e^(rt)","w":["P₀ + rt","K/(1 + Ce^(−rt))","rP₀t"],"why":"Unchecked growth: proportional-slope."},
 {"q":"Yeast doubles every 90 minutes. r per hour?","a":"ln 2 / 1.5","w":["1.5 / ln 2","2 / 1.5","ln 1.5"],"why":"Doubling-time read backwards."},
 {"q":"P′ = rP(1 − P/K). Its accepted name?","a":"the logistic","w":["unchecked growth","the law of cooling (Newton's)","first order"],"why":"Growth with a ceiling K."},
 {"q":"Logistic growth is fastest where?","a":"at P = K/2","w":["at the start","at P = K","at P = K/4"],"why":"Fastest-growth."},
 {"q":"P → K, so P = K at some time?","a":"no: approached, never reached","w":["yes","yes, at t = ln C / r","no: it passes K"],"why":"Ce^(−rt) > 0 for every t."},
 {"q":"P = 800/(1 + 15e^(−0.5t)). What is 800?","a":"K, the ceiling","w":["P₀","r","C"],"why":"As t grows, P → 800."},
 {"q":"P = K/(1 + Ce^(−rt)). C = ?","a":"(K − P₀)/P₀","w":["P₀/(K − P₀)","K/P₀","K − P₀"],"why":"Logistic-solution at t = 0."},
 {"q":"A fit to a few early years. Is K seen?","a":"barely: early on it looks exponential","w":["yes, clearly","yes, as P₀","no, and r is not seen either"],"why":"P ≪ K, so P′ ≈ rP."},
 {"q":"P₀ > K. What does the logistic do?","a":"P falls toward K from above","w":["logistic-solution fails","P grows without bound","P falls to 0"],"why":"The same steps with ln(P − K)."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["19·bio"]=[{"q": "Energy released down a chain of carriers. What does it depend on?", "a": "only the first and the last carrier", "w": ["every step on the route", "the number of steps", "the speed of the chain"], "why": "The route-free technique."},
 {"q": "Each step banks at most m coins of size c. A drop D needs how many steps?", "a": "at least D/(mc)", "w": ["exactly D", "at most mc", "D times mc"], "why": "The whole-coins technique."},
 {"q": "Why not burn the sugar in one step?", "a": "one step can bank only a few coins; the rest is lost as heat", "w": ["burning releases no energy", "the route changes the total", "sugar cannot burn"], "why": "Banking in full needs many small steps."},
 {"q": "A coin carried across a membrane. What is it worth?", "a": "RT ln(ratio), plus its charge times the voltage", "w": ["only its charge times the voltage", "the ratio itself", "nothing: membranes store no work"], "why": "A membrane is a battery."},
 {"q": "Two routes from sugar to oxygen. Which releases more?", "a": "neither: the drop is the same", "w": ["the longer route", "the shorter route", "the faster route"], "why": "Only the ends count."},
 {"q": "The ratio across a membrane is 1, and there is no voltage. What is a coin worth?", "a": "nothing", "w": ["RT", "one coin", "the full drop"], "why": "ln 1 = 0."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["16·bio"]=[{"q": "F · D = Cl · ∫₀^∞ C(t) dt. Which technique?", "a": "dose-area", "w": ["net-change", "stretched-curve", "level"], "why": "The amount taken up is the clearance times the whole area under the blood level."},
 {"q": "Area 4 mg·h/L, clearance 5 L/h. Amount taken up?", "a": "20 mg", "w": ["4 mg", "0.8 mg", "1.25 mg"], "why": "mg·h/L times L/h is mg."},
 {"q": "The body clears Cl · C(t) mg an hour. What is Cl?", "a": "the clearance", "w": ["the dose", "the share absorbed", "the crest"], "why": "A fixed number of litres an hour, measured: a law with a range."},
 {"q": "C_k(t) = (1/k) C(t/k). Its whole area?", "a": "the same as C's", "w": ["k times C's", "C's over k", "k² times C's"], "why": "Stretched-curve: k times longer, k times lower."},
 {"q": "Two pills, the same area. Same effect?", "a": "no: crest and timing differ", "w": ["yes", "yes, if the dose is the same", "no: different amounts taken up"], "why": "The area says how much, not how high or when."},
 {"q": "Samples stop at 12 h. Is the area to 12 h the whole area?", "a": "no: there is a tail", "w": ["yes", "yes, if the level is small", "no: it is twice as much"], "why": "The last sample is not the end."},
 {"q": "Each strip uses the average of its two end levels. What sum is that?", "a": "the averaged strip sum", "w": ["the left sum", "the upper sum", "the right sum"], "why": "Left and right sums, halved and added."},
 {"q": "Alcohol, past small amounts, is cleared at a fixed amount an hour. Does dose-area hold?", "a": "no: its law fails", "w": ["yes", "yes, with a larger Cl", "only for the first hour"], "why": "Dose-area needs clearing in proportion to the level."},
 {"q": "The quick pill's crest is twice the slow one's. Did it take up more?", "a": "no: the same 20 mg", "w": ["yes, twice as much", "yes, a little more", "no: half as much"], "why": "Both have area 4 mg·h/L."},
 {"q": "Up to 3 h the area is 2.25 and Cl = 5. What is 11.25 mg?", "a": "the amount cleared so far", "w": ["the amount still in", "the amount taken up", "the dose"], "why": "Cleared so far is Cl times the area so far."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["21·bio"]=[{"q": "Seed n sits at reach c√n and turn 2πnα. What is this called on the page?", "a": "the seed-place technique", "w": ["the spoke technique", "the spiral-count technique", "the golden-turn technique"], "why": "Equal room for each seed gives c√n; a fixed turn gives 2πnα."},
 {"q": "Why is the reach of seed n proportional to √n?", "a": "n equal seeds fill a disc of area πR²", "w": ["the turn grows with n", "the arms are spirals", "the misses shrink"], "why": "πR² = ns, so R = c√n (21n)."},
 {"q": "α = 3/8 of a turn. How do the seeds lie?", "a": "on 8 spokes", "w": ["on 3 spokes", "in 8 curving arms", "in 3 curving arms"], "why": "Spoke technique: seed n + 8 is at seed n’s turn plus 3 whole turns."},
 {"q": "α = 0.41. What is the miss of 5?", "a": "+0.05", "w": ["−0.05", "2.05", "0.95"], "why": "5 × 0.41 = 2.05, less the nearest whole number 2."},
 {"q": "(1 − α)/α = 1/(1 − α). What does the guess give?", "a": "α ≈ 0.381966 of a turn", "w": ["α = 0.5 of a turn", "α ≈ 0.618034 of a turn", "α = 2/5 of a turn"], "why": "(1 − α)² = α, and the smaller root of α² − 3α + 1 = 0 is (3 − √5)/2."},
 {"q": "About how many degrees is the golden turn?", "a": "137.5°", "w": ["144°", "222.5°", "120°"], "why": "0.381966 × 360° ≈ 137.5°."},
 {"q": "For the golden turn, the miss of 34 is −0.01316. What is the miss of 55?", "a": "+0.00813", "w": ["−0.00813", "+0.01316", "−0.02129"], "why": "Golden-turn technique: divide by r ≈ 1.618 and change the sign."},
 {"q": "Seeds 34 apart have a miss below 0, and seeds 55 apart above 0. What does the page conclude?", "a": "34 arms lean one way, 55 the other", "w": ["both sets of arms lean one way", "the seeds lie on 89 spokes", "the turn is a simple fraction"], "why": "Spiral-count technique: the sign of the miss gives the lean."},
 {"q": "α = 0.38. What happens far out?", "a": "50 straight spokes", "w": ["34 and 55 arms", "8 straight spokes", "no pattern at all"], "why": "0.38 = 19/50, so the miss of 50 is 0."},
 {"q": "A line reads: the miss of q is qα less the whole number below. What is wrong?", "a": "it is the nearest whole number", "w": ["it is qα less α", "it is the whole number above", "nothing: it is allowed"], "why": "The miss can be below 0, and its sign gives the lean."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["2·bio"]=[{"q": "J35 swims with J pod. Which mark joins J35 and J pod?", "a": "∈", "w": ["⊆", "=", "∪"], "why": "J35 is one orca, a member of the pod."},
 {"q": "Which mark joins J pod and the orcas?", "a": "⊆", "w": ["∈", "=", "∉"], "why": "A set of orcas is part of the orcas, not one of them."},
 {"q": "“A whale is a fish”, read with sets: which claim?", "a": "the whales ⊆ the fish", "w": ["whales ∈ the fish", "the fish ⊆ the whales", "the whales = the fish"], "why": "A claim about parts: every whale is a member of the fish."},
 {"q": "f = {x ∈ a : x breathes with gills ∧ x has fins}. Is the orca in f?", "a": "No: it breathes with lungs", "w": ["Yes: it has fins", "Yes: it lives in the water", "Not settled"], "why": "The orca fails the gills part of the condition."},
 {"q": "Two sifts of the animals by different conditions, both called “fish”. Are they the same set?", "a": "Only if they have the same members", "w": ["Yes: they share a name", "Yes: both are sifts of a", "No: two sifts never match"], "why": "Same-members: a set is nothing but its members, whatever it is called."},
 {"q": "gₐ = {x ∈ l : x = a ∨ x d a}. What is it?", "a": "A whole branch: a and every descendant", "w": ["The ancestors of a", "The descendants of a, without a", "A sift by features"], "why": "It keeps an ancestor and every one of its descendants."},
 {"q": "y ∈ gₐ and x d y, so x ∈ gₐ. Which technique?", "a": "Keep-every-descendant", "w": ["The chain technique for part of", "Sift-is-part", "Classes never overlap"], "why": "A whole branch never lets a descendant of its members out."},
 {"q": "Which property of “is descended from” does keep-every-descendant use?", "a": "Transitive", "w": ["Reflexive", "Symmetric", "Same members"], "why": "x d y and y d a give x d a."},
 {"q": "Is the fish by gills and fins a whole branch?", "a": "No: it leaves out descendants, such as whales", "w": ["Yes: fish share an ancestor", "Yes: every fish has gills", "No: it holds no lungfish"], "why": "Every whole branch holding the salmon and the lungfish holds the whale."},
 {"q": "“The Southern Resident orcas are endangered.” What is it a claim about?", "a": "The set, not each orca", "w": ["Each orca in the set", "J35 alone", "The orcas being part of the whales"], "why": "Two sizes of truth: what is true of the kind need not be true of each member."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["24·bio"]=[{"q": "θ′ = pxⁿ(1 − θ) − qθ. Where does θ settle?", "a": "xⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ), with Kⁿ = q/p", "w": ["x/(K + x)", "Kⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ)", "pxⁿ/q"], "why": "The binding technique; the state holds since the slope is −(pxⁿ + q)."}, {"q": "xⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ) at x = K. What share is caught?", "a": "one half", "w": ["one quarter", "n/(n + 1)", "all of it"], "why": "Kⁿ/(2Kⁿ) = ½, whatever n is."}, {"q": "From 10% caught to 90% caught. How much more signal does it take?", "a": "81^(1/n) times as much", "w": ["81 times, always", "9n times", "n times"], "why": "The steepness technique: x₉₀/x₁₀ = 81^(1/n)."}, {"q": "xⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ) with n near 2.8, K near 26.8 mmHg. What is it?", "a": "haemoglobin’s oxygen curve", "w": ["a cooling curve", "a logistic population", "a thermostat’s droop"], "why": "Steep catching lets blood load in the lungs and unload in muscle."}, {"q": "u′ = a/(1 + v²) − u, v′ = a/(1 + u²) − v, a > 2. What do the two genes make?", "a": "a switch with two states that hold", "w": ["a clock", "one state that holds", "nothing that holds"], "why": "The switch technique: uv = 1, u + v = a."}, {"q": "Two genes that stop each other, n = 2. For which a is there a switch?", "a": "a > 2", "w": ["any a > 0", "a < 2", "a = 4 only"], "why": "The held states (a ± √(a² − 4))/2 need a² > 4."}, {"q": "Three genes in a ring, each stopping the next. How many states are left alone?", "a": "exactly one", "w": ["three", "none", "two"], "why": "h = f∘f∘f falls, so h(x) − x crosses 0 once (in-between values)."}, {"q": "f′(L) for f(x) = a/(1 + xⁿ) at its own L. What is it?", "a": "−n(1 − L/a)", "w": ["−n", "−L/a", "−na"], "why": "Use 1 + Lⁿ = a/L."}, {"q": "A ring of three with n = 1. Can it take turns?", "a": "no: n(1 − L/a) < 1", "w": ["yes, always", "yes, for large a", "only in the relay reading"], "why": "A clock of this kind needs genes caught several at a time."}, {"q": "Cut off from daylight, a cycle starts 18 minutes earlier each day. What is its own period?", "a": "23.7 hours", "w": ["24 hours", "24.3 hours", "18 hours"], "why": "The own-period technique: a steady drift means a period of its own."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["28·bio"]=[{"q": "x′ = x(a − by), y′ = y(−c + dx). The rest with both alive is …", "a": "(c/d, a/b)", "w": ["(a/b, c/d)", "(a/d, c/b)", "(0, 0)"], "why": "The crossed-rest technique (27·then.3): each bracket must be 0."}, {"q": "With u = x − x*, w = y − y*, the rules give uw′ − wu′ = dy u² + bx w². So the vector from the rest …", "a": "always turns the same way", "w": ["always heads in to the rest", "stops turning on the axes", "turns back and forth"], "why": "The always-round technique: the sum is above 0 away from the rest."}, {"q": "The vector from the rest always turns. Does that make the paths close?", "a": "No: turning paths can still spiral in or out", "w": ["Yes, always", "Only if T > 0", "Only if Δ < 0"], "why": "Turning is not closing; with crowding the paths turn and spiral in."}, {"q": "Along every path of the rules, V = dx − c ln x + by − a ln y …", "a": "never changes", "w": ["only falls", "only rises", "doubles every loop"], "why": "The kept-quantity technique: V′ = (dx − c)(a − by) + (by − a)(dx − c) = 0."}, {"q": "V′ = V_x x′ + V_y y′ is licensed by …", "a": "the roads-add technique (28c)", "w": ["the one-knob technique (28a)", "the crossed-rest technique", "the sum-and-determinant technique"], "why": "Both counts move in time, so the slope of V adds along both roads."}, {"q": "Each level curve of V above its lowest value is …", "a": "one loop round the rest", "w": ["a straight line", "two separate loops", "a spiral"], "why": "The level-loop technique: V rises along every ray from the rest, with no ceiling."}, {"q": "Over any loop of the rules, the average number of rabbits is …", "a": "c/d, the rest value", "w": ["half the peak", "the start value", "a/b"], "why": "The loop-average technique: integrate (ln y)′ = dx − c over one time round."}, {"q": "Both counts are trapped steadily at an extra rate h. On average there are …", "a": "more rabbits and fewer foxes", "w": ["fewer of both", "more of both", "more foxes and fewer rabbits"], "why": "Averages (c + h)/d and (a − h)/b: Volterra’s principle."}, {"q": "A cull of rabbits made while they are below c/d …", "a": "moves every later swing outward", "w": ["stops the swings", "moves the swings inward", "leaves the loop as it was"], "why": "The wrong-moment technique: dx − c ln x falls below c/d, so V rises."}, {"q": "Add crowding: x′ = ax − ex² − bxy with e > 0. The paths …", "a": "spiral in to the rest", "w": ["still close into loops", "spiral out to the axes", "stop turning"], "why": "The crowding-spiral technique: W′ = −(e/d)(dx − c)² ≤ 0."}, {"q": "The closed loops of these rules rest on an exact balance. So they are …", "a": "a fact about these rules, not a forecast for hares", "w": ["proof that hares cycle", "wrong", "true for every predator and prey"], "why": "The green box: check that a result survives a small change in the rules."}];
