(window.LADDER_CARDS["14·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["14·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"14·bio.1.1","q":"What does x² = 2Dt say, and why is there no speed of diffusion?","a":"the typical distance spread grows with time so that its square is 2Dt; the time for a distance is x²/(2D), whose slope x/D grows with x, so every further micrometre costs more than the last","w":["the distance spread is 2Dt; the speed is 2D","the distance is fixed for each molecule; the speed is 0","the time is 2Dx; the speed grows with distance"],"why":"10 µm takes 0.025 s and 1 cm about 7 hours. There is only a time for each distance."},
 {"u":"14·bio.2.1","q":"Where is oxygen least in a living sheet, and how thick can the sheet be?","a":"at the middle, where c(0) = c₀ − qL²/(2D); the sheet stays supplied while that is at least 0, so the half-thickness can be at most √(2Dc₀/q)","w":["at the faces; any thickness is supplied","at a quarter of the way in; the half-thickness can be at most c₀/q","everywhere the same; the sheet can be at most 1 mm"],"why":"c′(x) = (q/D)x is 0 only at 0, and c″ = q/D > 0: a bottom. The middle gets worse as the sheet gets thicker."},
 {"u":"14·bio.3.1","q":"What does putting L_max back into the first premise show?","a":"the time for oxygen to spread from a face to the middle of the thickest sheet is c₀/q, the time the tissue would take to use up the oxygen it holds; D has dropped out","w":["the time to arrive is twice the time to run out","the thickest sheet depends only on D","oxygen never reaches the middle"],"why":"t = L_max²/(2D) = (2Dc₀/q)/(2D) = c₀/q. The thickest sheet is where arriving and using up take the same time."},
 {"u":"14·bio.4.1","q":"How does a ball-shaped cell differ from the sheet?","a":"its settled oxygen curve has a 6 where the sheet had a 2, so the least value is still at the centre and the greatest radius is √(6Dc₀/q)","w":["its oxygen is least at the skin","it has no greatest radius","its curve has a 2 where the sheet had a 6, so it must be thinner"],"why":"The same techniques apply. A bacterium using oxygen fast could be about 18 µm in radius, and is about 1 µm."},
 {"u":"14·bio.5.1","q":"Why is “twice as far, twice as long” a move to watch out for?","a":"the time grows with the square of the distance, so twice as far takes four times as long","w":["twice as far takes half as long","the time does not depend on the distance","twice as far takes eight times as long"],"why":"t = x²/(2D). That is why a large animal needs lungs, gills or folded guts that a small one does without."});
/* end phase F und_r14 */
/* phase F und_r16: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["16·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["16·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"16·bio.1.1","q":"What does the dose-area technique say, and how do the body's books prove it?","a":"the amount taken up, F·D, is the clearance times the whole area under the blood level; Q′ = a − Cl·C, and since Q starts at 0 and heads back to 0, all that came in equals all that was cleared","w":["the amount taken up is the highest level times the clearance; the books are read at the peak","the amount taken up is the area under the level divided by the clearance; Q′ = a + Cl·C","the amount taken up is the dose D whatever the level; the body clears nothing"],"why":"Net-change gives Q(T) = ∫₀ᵀ a − Cl ∫₀ᵀ C. As T grows, Q → 0 and ∫a → F·D."},
 {"u":"16·bio.2.1","q":"For the pill C(t) = 8t/(1 + t)³ with Cl = 5 L/h, how much is taken up, and when is the level highest?","a":"the area is 4 mg·h/L, so 20 mg is taken up; the level is highest half an hour in, at 32/27 ≈ 1.19 mg/L","w":["the area is 8 mg·h/L, so 40 mg is taken up; the level is highest an hour in, at 1 mg/L","the area is 4 mg·h/L, so 0.8 mg is taken up; the level is highest at the start","the area has no finite value, so the amount cannot be told; the level is highest half an hour in"],"why":"With u = 1 + t the integral is 8∫₁^∞ (u − 1)/u³ du = 8(1 − ½) = 4. Dose-area gives 5 × 4 = 20 mg."},
 {"u":"16·bio.3.1","q":"How does a lab get the area from samples, and why is the last sample not the end?","a":"by the averaged strip sum, each strip taking the average of the levels at its two ends, about 3.31 to 12 h; the drug is still in the blood after it, so a guessed tail, about 0.53, is added","w":["by adding the samples up, about 4.45; the last sample is the end, since the level is then 0","by the highest sample times the hours, about 14.2; the tail is left out as too small","by the left sum alone, about 3.31; the tail is known exactly, since the curve is drawn"],"why":"The tail is taken as k/t², and ∫₁₂^∞ k/t² dt = k/12. It is about a seventh of the whole area, so a report says how it was filled in."},
 {"u":"16·bio.4.1","q":"What does stretched-curve say, and what can the area not tell apart?","a":"a level curve stretched to last k times longer and stand k times lower keeps its area, with its crest k times later and lower; the area cannot tell a quick pill from a slow one","w":["a curve stretched k times longer has k times the area; the area cannot tell two drugs apart","a curve stretched k times longer keeps its crest; the area cannot tell the dose","a curve stretched k times longer and k times higher keeps its area; the area tells the pills apart"],"why":"With s = t/k, ∫ (1/k)C(t/k) dt = ∫ C(s) ds. Both pills give 20 mg, but only the quick one passes 1 mg/L."},
 {"u":"16·bio.5.1","q":"Why is “the higher peak took up more” a move to watch out for?","a":"the amount taken up is the clearance times the area, not the height: the quick and slow pills have different peaks and the same 20 mg","w":["the lower peak always takes up more","the peak gives the amount only after it is doubled","no pill's amount can be found from its level curve"],"why":"Stretched-curve moves the crest and keeps the area. Dose-area reads the amount from the area alone."});
/* end phase F und_r16 */
/* phase F und_r17: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["17·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["17·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"17·bio.1.1","q":"What does unchecked growth look like, and what would one bacterium dividing every 20 minutes become in a day?","a":"P′ = rP, so P = P₀e^(rt), doubling every ln 2 / r; in 24 hours, 2⁷² ≈ 4.7 × 10²¹ cells, some 4700 tonnes from one cell, if nothing stopped it","w":["P′ = r, so P = P₀ + rt; 72 cells in a day","P′ = rP, so P = P₀e^(rt); 2²⁴ cells, about a gram","P′ = rP², so P doubles sooner each time; 72² cells"],"why":"Three doublings an hour for 24 hours is 72 doublings. It is proportional-slope and doubling-time."},
 {"u":"17·bio.2.1","q":"What does logistic-solution say, and when is growth fastest?","a":"if P′ = rP(1 − P/K) and 0 < P₀ < K, then P(t) = K/(1 + Ce^(−rt)) with C = (K − P₀)/P₀; P′ is greatest when P = K/2, at t = ln C / r, where it is rK/4","w":["P(t) = P₀e^(rt) until it reaches K, then stops; growth is fastest at the start","P(t) = K(1 − e^(−rt)); growth is fastest when P = K","P(t) = K/(1 + Ce^(−rt)) with C = K/P₀; growth is fastest at t = 0"],"why":"ln P − ln(K − P) has slope r, by 17k's split read backwards. Completing the square puts the greatest P′ at K/2."},
 {"u":"17·bio.3.1","q":"Why is \"logistic growth is fastest at the start\" a move to watch out for?","a":"growth is fastest at P = K/2; the start is slow when P₀ is small","w":["growth is fastest as P reaches K","growth is the same at every time","growth is fastest at the start only when r > 1"],"why":"Fastest-growth: P′ = (r/K)P(K − P) is greatest at the middle height."});
/* end phase F und_r17 */
/* phase F und_r19: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["19·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["19·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"19·bio.1.1","q":"How does the electron ledger balance the burning of sugar, and why is the sugar side the first thing the cell takes apart?","a":"the six carbons go from 0 to +4 and give up 24 electrons, each O₂ takes 4, so 6 O₂ are needed; the plant built the sugar side last, and by socks and shoes the undo of a string of steps does the last one first","w":["each carbon gives up 4 electrons and each O₂ takes 2, so 12 O₂ are needed; the sugar side is the largest","the six carbons give up 24 electrons and each O₂ takes 4, so 6 O₂ are needed; the cell undoes the plant's steps in the order the plant did them","the atoms are counted first and the electrons follow; the sugar side holds the most oxygen"],"why":"6 · (4 − 0) = 24 electrons given up, 24 / 4 = 6 oxygen molecules, and then the atoms are counted."},
 {"u":"19·bio.2.1","q":"What does the route-free technique say, and which earlier technique collapses the sum?","a":"the energy released down a chain of carriers depends only on the first and the last; the steps nF(E_j − E_{j−1}) add to nF(E_k − E₀) by collapsing-sum (15g)","w":["the energy released grows with the number of carriers; the steps add by the geometric sum","the energy released depends only on the first and the last carrier; the steps cancel by socks and shoes (19c)","every carrier releases the same energy; the sum is k times one step"],"why":"So the whole sugar releases about 2870 kJ per mole, the same whether burned or breathed."},
 {"u":"19·bio.3.1","q":"What does the whole-coins technique say, and why is the slow fire slow on purpose?","a":"if each step can bank at most m coins of size c, a drop D needs at least D/(mc) steps to be banked in full; a fire is one step with nothing coupled and banks none, and many small steps let almost every drop pay a whole coin","w":["a drop D can be banked in one step as D/c coins; the slow fire is slow because the carriers are cold","each step banks exactly m coins, so k steps bank more than D; the slow fire releases more energy than a fast one","a drop D needs at most D/(mc) steps; the slow fire is slow so that less heat is released in all"],"why":"The chain from NADH banks 10 proton coins of about 19 kJ: 190 of 220 kJ, about 86%."},
 {"u":"19·bio.4.1","q":"What does the ratio-coin technique say, and why is the work to squeeze a dissolved substance a log?","a":"a coin across a membrane is worth RT ln(ratio), plus its charge times the voltage; the pressure is RT/V, and the area under 1/V is ln (17a)","w":["a coin is worth RT times the difference of the two concentrations; the pressure is fixed, so the area is a rectangle","a coin is worth RT ln(ratio), and the voltage adds nothing; the pressure is RT · V","a coin is worth its charge times the voltage only; the log comes from counting protons in pH units"],"why":"With 0.75 pH units and 0.15 V across, 4.4 + 14.5 gives about 19 kJ per mole of protons."},
 {"u":"19·bio.5.1","q":"Why is \"more steps release more energy\" a move to watch out for?","a":"by route-free the sum of the steps collapses to its two ends, so every route releases the same; more steps change how much is banked, not how much is released","w":["more steps release less energy, since each step loses heat","more steps release more energy only when each banks a whole coin","the steps release nothing; only the last carrier releases energy"],"why":"The fire and the chain both release about 2870 kJ per mole of sugar; the fire banks none of it."});
/* end phase F und_r19 */
/* phase F und_r21: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["21·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["21·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"21·bio.1.1","q":"What does the seed-place technique say, and why is the reach of seed n equal to c√n?","a":"seed n sits at reach c√n and turn 2πnα; each seed takes about the same room s, so the first n seeds fill a disc of area ns, and πR² = ns gives R = c√n with c = √(s/π)","w":["seed n sits at reach cn and turn 2πα; each seed is pushed out one step at a time","seed n sits at reach c√n and turn 2πnα; the reach is c√n because the turn grows as n","seed n sits at reach c/n and turn 2πnα; the seeds crowd toward the rim"],"why":"Only the part of nα past a whole number matters, since whole turns change nothing."},
 {"u":"21·bio.2.1","q":"What does the spoke technique say, and what is the miss of q?","a":"a turn of p/q of the whole puts every seed on just q spokes, and the wedges between them widen as the head grows; the miss of q is qα less the whole number nearest to it","w":["a turn of p/q puts every seed on p spokes; the miss of q is qα less the whole number below it","a turn of p/q spreads the seeds evenly; the miss of q is α − p/q","a turn of p/q puts every seed on q spokes, and the wedges close as the head grows; the miss of q is q/α"],"why":"Seed n + q sits at the turn of seed n plus p whole turns."},
 {"u":"21·bio.3.1","q":"Which share of the turn is the golden turn, and what does the golden-turn technique say about its misses?","a":"α = (3 − √5)/2 ≈ 0.382 of a turn, about 137.5°, where the larger piece is to the smaller as the whole is to the larger; each miss of a rabbit count is the last one divided by r ≈ 1.618 with its sign changed, so the misses never reach 0","w":["α = 0.38 = 19/50; its misses are all 0","α = (1 + √5)/2 of a turn; each miss is the last one times r","α = (3 − √5)/2; its misses reach 0 at every rabbit count"],"why":"The miss of F_k is (−1)ᵏ r⁻ᵏ, by induction."},
 {"u":"21·bio.4.1","q":"What does the spiral-count technique say, and which pairs of arms does a head show?","a":"seeds q apart form q arms, which lean the way the sign of the miss of q says; for the golden turn neighbouring rabbit counts lean opposite ways, so a head shows pairs such as 34 and 55, or 55 and 89 on a larger one","w":["seeds q apart form α arms; a head shows any two numbers of arms","seeds q apart form q arms, all leaning one way; a head shows 34 and 68","the arms come from the plant twisting as it grows; a head shows 50 spokes"],"why":"The miss of 34 is −0.01316 and the miss of 55 is +0.00813."},
 {"u":"21·bio.5.1","q":"Why is \"the arms come from the plant twisting\" a move to watch out for?","a":"the arms are seeds q apart, each placed by one fixed turn; nothing twists","w":["the plant twists, but only in large heads","the arms are drawn by the eye and are not in the seeds at all","the arms come from seeds growing at different speeds"],"why":"Seeds q apart sit one miss of q apart in turn, and a little farther out: an arm."});
/* end phase F und_r21 */
/* phase F und_r24: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["24·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["24·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"24·bio.1.1","q":"A protein catches n copies of a signal at once. What share ends up caught, and why does that state hold?","a":"xⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ), half when x = K, whatever the start; the rate rule θ′ = pxⁿ(1 − θ) − qθ has slope −(pxⁿ + q) < 0 in θ, so the state left alone holds (24k)","w":["x/(K + x) for every n; it holds because θ is a share","xⁿ/(Kⁿ + xⁿ); it holds because the slope in θ is positive","n·x/K; it holds only from a start at 0"],"why":"With n = 4 the share hardly moves below K and is nearly full a little above it."},
 {"u":"24·bio.2.1","q":"How much more signal takes a protein from 10% caught to 90% caught, and what does a fitted n that is not whole measure?","a":"81 times as much when n = 1, and only 81^(1/n) times when n copies are caught at once; a fitted n that is not whole measures how steep the switch is, since the seats do not all fill at once","w":["9 times for every n; a fitted n counts the seats","81ⁿ times; a fitted n measures the protein's size","81/n times; a fitted n that is not whole is a slip of measurement"],"why":"For haemoglobin, n ≈ 2.8 gives about 4.8 times."},
 {"u":"24·bio.3.1","q":"Two genes hold each other down, with α > 2 and n = 2. How many states hold, and what is the relay reading worth?","a":"two, one with each gene on; the third, with both equal, tips; the relay reading, which follows one protein through the other and back, is a guess: here the full two-rule check, held in trust, gives the same answer","w":["one, with both genes half on; the relay reading is a proof","three, all holding; the relay reading is not used","two; the relay reading is a proof, and the two-rule check disagrees"],"why":"A pulse that pulls one protein off its gene flips the switch, and it stays flipped."},
 {"u":"24·bio.4.1","q":"What do three genes in a ring do when their switches are steep enough, and why does n = 1 never tick?","a":"they have one state left alone, and it pushes, so the genes take turns for ever; the relay reading asks n(1 − L/α) > 1, and 1 − L/α < 1, so n = 1 fails: a clock of this kind needs genes caught several copies at a time","w":["they settle at one state that holds; n = 1 ticks fastest","they have two states that hold; n = 1 never ticks because α is too large","they take turns for every n; n = 1 ticks slowly"],"why":"The two-rule check, held in trust, asks for more: n(1 − L/α) > 2."},
 {"u":"24·bio.5.1","q":"How does a cycle cut off from daylight tell a clock inside from a reply to the sun?","a":"a reply to the sun would keep exactly 24 hours; a cycle that drifts steadily against the sun, P − 24 hours later each day, keeps a period of its own","w":["a clock inside would keep exactly 24 hours; a reply would drift","both would stop in the dark","a clock inside would speed up without light; a reply would slow"],"why":"People in dim, steady light kept about 24.2 hours, drifting about 11 minutes a day."},
 {"u":"24·bio.6.1","q":"What is out of place in “the relay takes turns, so the cell keeps time”?","a":"the relay reading is a guess; the rules on rates ask for more: n(1 − L/α) > 2","w":["the relay never takes turns","the rules on rates ask for less: n(1 − L/α) > ½","nothing: the relay reading is the two-rule check"],"why":"Passing the weaker test is not passing the stronger one."});
/* end phase F und_r24 */
/* phase F und_r28: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["28·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["28·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"28·bio.1.1","q":"What does the always-round technique say, and why can the slope grid at the rest not settle whether the paths close?","a":"away from the rest, the vector from the rest to the counts always turns the same way, from more rabbits toward more foxes, and never stops; but turning is not closing, a path can turn for ever and spiral in or out, and the slope grid sits on the border line, where it cannot tell","w":["the vector from the rest turns one way and then back; the slope grid shows the paths close","the vector never turns; the slope grid shows a saddle","the vector always turns, so the paths close; the slope grid agrees"],"why":"The foxes peak a quarter turn after the rabbits."},
 {"u":"28·bio.2.1","q":"What does the kept-quantity technique say, and how is V′ = 0 checked?","a":"along every path, V = dx − c ln x + by − a ln y never changes, so each path stays on one level curve of V; the check is the roads-add technique: V′ = V_x x′ + V_y y′, and putting in the rules makes the two terms cancel","w":["V = xy never changes; it is checked by trying a few paths on a machine","V = dx + by never changes; it is checked at the rest only","V only falls along a path; it is checked by the grid test"],"why":"A kept quantity is a guess that has to be checked."},
 {"u":"28·bio.3.1","q":"What does the level-loop technique say, and why does each ray from the rest meet a level curve of V exactly once?","a":"every level curve of V above its lowest value is one loop round the rest, and every path other than the rest goes round its loop again and again; V has its one bottom at the rest and rises all the way along every ray from it, so each value above the lowest is met once","w":["every level curve is a spiral into the rest; each ray meets it endlessly often","every level curve is a loop, but paths cross from loop to loop; each ray meets a curve twice","only small level curves are loops; V falls along rays from the rest"],"why":"The border line is settled by a quantity the grid never saw."},
 {"u":"28·bio.4.1","q":"What does the loop-average technique say, and what does steady trapping of both do to the averages?","a":"over every loop the average number of rabbits is c/d and of foxes a/b, however wide the loop; steady killing of both lowers the rabbits’ growth a and raises the foxes’ loss c, so it leaves more rabbits on average and fewer foxes","w":["the averages depend on the width of the loop; trapping both lowers both","the averages are a/b rabbits and c/d foxes; trapping both raises the foxes","the averages are the midpoints of the swings; trapping both changes nothing"],"why":"Spraying that killed pest and beetle alike brought the pest back in force."},
 {"u":"28·bio.5.1","q":"What does the wrong-moment technique say, and why does a cull of rabbits at or below c/d land on a larger loop?","a":"a cull of rabbits made at or below their average c/d moves every later swing outward, and one made above c/d that leaves them no lower than c/d moves it inward; dx − c ln x has its bottom at x = c/d, so pushing x further below it raises V, and a higher level curve is a wider loop","w":["every cull of rabbits moves the swings inward; fewer rabbits means a smaller loop","a cull at the peak moves the swings outward; V rises with x everywhere","a cull changes only the next swing; the loop afterwards is the old one"],"why":"A third culled at the low gives a peak a third higher, nine years on."},
 {"u":"28·bio.6.1","q":"What does the crowding-spiral technique say, and what does it show about the loops of 28·bio.3?","a":"with any crowding e above 0, W only falls along a path, so no path but the rest can close and the paths spiral in; the loops rest on an exact balance in the rules and vanish under the smallest change, so they show what those rules do and not why real animals cycle","w":["with crowding the loops grow wider; the loops are a sturdy fact about the world","with crowding the paths spiral out; the loops of 28·bio.3 are unchanged","crowding has no effect until e is large; the loops hold for small e"],"why":"Check whether a result survives a small change before reading it as a fact."},
 {"u":"28·bio.7.1","q":"What is wrong with “the vector always turns, so the paths close”?","a":"turning is not closing: a path that turns for ever can spiral in to the rest or out toward the axes; closing needed the kept quantity V","w":["the vector does not always turn","paths close exactly when the turning is steady","nothing; a path that turns must return"],"why":"With crowding the vector still turns, and the paths spiral in."},
 {"u":"28·bio.8.1","q":"What does an age grid hold along its top row and just under its middle, what does the age-grid technique say about the long run, and what does the owls’ 0.984 mean?","a":"births for each age along the top row and the shares that live on to the next age just under the middle; exactly one stretch λ is a positive number, with age vector (1, s₁/λ, s₁s₂/λ²), and when it is the largest in size the population grows by λ a year in those shares, whatever its start; 0.984 means the owls shrink by about 1.6% a year","w":["survival along the top row and births under the middle; every stretch is positive; 0.984 is the share of owls that live","births along the top row and survival under the middle; the population keeps the shares of its start; 0.984 is the share of adults","births down the middle; the population settles to equal shares; 0.984 means the owls grow by 98.4% a year"],"why":"Raise the young’s survival and λ passes 1."});
/* end phase F und_r28 */
/* phase F und_r30: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30·bio.1.1","q":"What does the one-round-rest technique say, and what alone can make a gene spread?","a":"if copies join at random, one round of mixing sets the shares of AA, Aa and aa at p², 2pq and q², and they stay there; mixing alone changes nothing, so a gene spreads only when something else acts, such as an edge in leaving young or a small population","w":["the shares head to p², 2pq and q² over many rounds; mixing alone slowly spreads the commoner allele","the shares settle at 1/4, 1/2 and 1/4 after one round; mixing alone removes rare alleles","one round sets the shares at p², 2pq and q²; a gene whose form shows in a single dose then spreads by mixing alone"],"why":"A form that shows in a single dose does not take over by that alone."},
 {"u":"30·bio.2.1","q":"What does the odds-growth technique say, and why does an edge that shows only in a double dose spread a rare allele so slowly?","a":"when each copy adds the same edge s, the odds p/q grow by the factor 1 + s every generation; a rare allele sits almost always in a single dose, where an edge that needs two copies does not show, so selection hardly sees it","w":["the share p grows by the factor 1 + s every generation; a double-dose edge is slow because it is half as strong","the odds p/q grow by s every generation; a double-dose edge is slow because double doses leave fewer young","the odds grow by the factor 1 + s; a double-dose edge spreads a rare allele faster, since two copies carry twice the edge"],"why":"At p = 0.01 and s = 0.02 the share rises by 0.0002 a generation when each copy counts, and by 0.000002 when only a double dose counts."},
 {"u":"30·bio.3.1","q":"What does the uphill-share technique say, and where does the balanced-share technique rest the share of A?","a":"selection moves the share p in the direction in which the average fitness w̄ rises, so p rests only where the slope of w̄ is 0 or at an end; when the mixed kind leaves the most young, both alleles are kept and the share of A rests at t/(s + t), with s and t the shortfalls of AA and aa","w":["selection moves p toward 1/2; when the mixed kind does best the share of A rests at 1/2","selection moves p downhill on w̄; when the mixed kind does best one allele is lost","selection moves p uphill on w̄; when the mixed kind does best the share of A rests at s/(s + t), the larger shortfall keeping the larger share"],"why":"The allele whose double dose falls short by more is kept at the smaller share."},
 {"u":"30·bio.4.1","q":"What do the shrinking-mix and fix-chance techniques say about drift?","a":"in a population of N, drift shrinks the mixed share 2pq by the factor 1 − 1/(2N) each generation on average; with drift alone an allele ends up in every copy with chance equal to its starting share, and otherwise is lost","w":["drift keeps 2pq fixed on average; an allele ends up in every copy with chance 1/2","drift shrinks 2pq by the factor 1 − 1/(2N); an allele with no edge is always lost in the end","drift grows 2pq by the factor 1 + 1/(2N); an allele ends up in every copy with chance equal to its share"],"why":"The average share does not move under drift, and the walk must end at 0 or 1."},
 {"u":"30·bio.5.1","q":"Why is “a new allele with no edge spreads half the time” a move to watch out for?","a":"an allele’s chance of ending up in every copy is its starting share, and a single new copy among N plants starts at 1/(2N), not 1/2","w":["a new allele with no edge is always lost","the chance is 1/N, since each plant has one copy","nothing is wrong; with no edge the two ends are equally likely"],"why":"In 50 plants, 1 time in 100."},
 {"u":"30·bio.6.1","q":"Why are two genes on one strand sent out mixed only by an odd number of swaps, and what does the odd-crossing technique say?","a":"an even number of swaps puts each gene back with its old partner; with swaps a rare count of average m, the share sent out mixed is r = (1 − e^(−2m))/2, which never reaches 1/2; averages of counts add along the strand, so map lengths m add, while r, bent by the formula, does not","w":["any swap mixes them; r = m, so both r and the map lengths add","an odd number of swaps puts each gene back; r = (1 + e^(−2m))/2, which never falls below 1/2","an even number puts each gene back; r = 1 − e^(−m), which heads to 1, so far genes are always sent out mixed"],"why":"m = −(1/2) ln(1 − 2r)."},
 {"u":"30·bio.7.1","q":"Why does a form on the X show in men at its share q but in women at q², and what does the X-share technique say about a mother who carries one copy?","a":"a man has one X, one draw, and a woman needs the form on both of hers, two draws; a mother with one copy passes it to half her sons, who show it, and to half her daughters, who carry it","w":["men show it at q² and women at q; a carrying mother passes it to all her sons","men and women both show it at q; a carrying mother passes it to half her sons only","men show it at q and women at 2q; a carrying mother passes it to half her daughters, who show it"],"why":"Men show it 1/q times as often as women."});
/* end phase F und_r30 */
/* phase F und_r33: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["33·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["33·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"33·bio.1.1","q":"What does the uptake-curve technique say, which premise of 14·bio does it close, and why can a ball be larger than a sheet is thick?","a":"settled oxygen is c₀ − (q/(2D))(L² − x²) in a sheet of half-thickness L and c₀ − (q/(6D))(R² − r²) in a ball; the sheet’s curve was taken as a premise on 14·bio and is now earned from DΔc = q; the largest supplied ball has R² = 6Dc₀/q against L² = 2Dc₀/q, √3 times as large, since three directions bring oxygen in","w":["settled oxygen is c₀ everywhere in both; it closes no premise; a ball and a sheet have the same limit","settled oxygen is c₀ − (q/(2D))(L² − x²) in a sheet and the same with R in a ball; a ball can be larger because it holds more oxygen","settled oxygen falls like e^(−x/L); it closes 14·bio’s premise that tissue uses no oxygen; a ball can be three times as large"],"why":"The round-ball technique turns the ball into a rod."},
 {"u":"33·bio.2.1","q":"What does the wiggle-test technique say, and why can spreading unsteady the mix only through the determinant?","a":"a wiggle cos kx grows or shrinks at the rates σ that are the stretches of A − k²D; the mix is unsteady to it when det(A − k²D) < 0 or the trace is above 0; spreading takes k²(D_u + D_v) off the trace, so a trace that was below 0 only falls further","w":["a wiggle grows at the rate k² whatever A is; spreading works through the trace alone","a wiggle grows at the stretches of A + k²D; spreading can only steady a mix","a wiggle grows when det A < 0; spreading does not enter the test at all"],"why":"The second slope of cos kx is −k² cos kx."},
 {"u":"33·bio.3.1","q":"What does the spreading-breaks-balance technique say, and which chemical must spread faster?","a":"a mix steady without spreading is made unsteady by it exactly when D_v f_u + D_u g_v > 2√(D_uD_v det A), with the fastest wiggles near k² = (D_v f_u + D_u g_v)/(2D_uD_v); the holder-back must spread faster than the maker: the maker stays local, and the holder-back spreads out and stops it nearby","w":["spreading unsteadies every mix; the two must spread at one speed","the condition is D_u = D_v; the maker must spread faster, to carry the pattern outward","the condition is D_v f_u + D_u g_v < 0; whichever chemical is rarer must spread faster"],"why":"With D_v ≤ D_u the band is empty: no pattern at all."},
 {"u":"33·bio.4.1","q":"What does the room-for-a-pattern technique say, and why can a thin tail carry rings but not spots?","a":"a strip of length ℓ grows a pattern only if some k = nπ/ℓ with n ≥ 1 lies in the growing band; round a thin tail there is room for no wiggle and along it for many, so the pattern can only be rings, while a wide flank has wiggles both ways that cross into spots","w":["a strip of any length grows every pattern; a tail has rings because its genes differ","a strip grows a pattern only if it is shorter than π over the band’s largest k; a tail is too long for spots","a strip grows a pattern only when n = 0 lies in the band; a tail has rings because it bends"],"why":"A very small animal has room for nothing and is plain."},
 {"u":"33·bio.5.1","q":"What does the drifting-share technique say, and why does the average share stay while the spread grows?","a":"after t generations the shares of many herds started at p₀ have average p₀ and spread p₀(1 − p₀)(1 − (1 − 1/(2N))ᵗ), early on about p₀(1 − p₀)t/(2N); each draw is a fair game, so the average never changes, while every generation adds its own chance spread","w":["the shares head back to p₀ with a spread that shrinks; drift undoes itself","the average falls toward 0 while the spread stays at p₀(1 − p₀)/(2N)","the average stays at p₀ and the spread grows like t² without limit"],"why":"Drift is the heat rule in gene shares."},
 {"u":"33·bio.6.1","q":"Why is “spreading always smooths, so it cannot make spots” a move to watch out for?","a":"one spreading chemical smooths; two that react and spread at different speeds can make an even mix unsteady, and a band of wiggles then grows","w":["spreading never smooths anything","spots come from the genes differing cell by cell","two chemicals spread exactly as one does"],"why":"33b is about one rule; here two are tied together."});
/* end phase F und_r33 */
/* phase F und_r34: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["34·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["34·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"34·bio.1.1","q":"What is the linking count of two closed strands, why does no twist, poke or slide change it, and why is it whole?","a":"half the sum of the signs of the crossings of A with B; a twist is a strand crossing itself, a poke adds two crossings of opposite sign, and a slide keeps each crossing’s strands and sign; it equals the sum of the signs where A passes over B, a whole number","w":["the number of all crossings in the drawing; moves change it by 2; it is whole because crossings are counted","half the signs of the crossings of A with B; a twist changes it by ½, which the next twist undoes; it can be a half","the sum of the signs of the crossings of A with itself; no move touches those; it is whole by its making"],"why":"Three rings can hang together with every pair at 0."},
 {"u":"34·bio.2.1","q":"Why is the linking count of a ring of DNA its number of turns, what is Lk₀, and which of Lk, Tw and Wr is fixed while the ring jostles?","a":"each full turn of the helix carries one strand once over and once under the other, two crossings of the same sign; Lk₀ = N/10.5 is the count of a relaxed ring of N base pairs; twist is the turns of the strands about the axis and coil the crossings of the axis with itself, and only their sum Lk = Tw + Wr is fixed","w":["each turn gives one crossing, so Lk is half the turns; Lk₀ = 10.5N; Tw is fixed and Lk changes as the ring jostles","each turn gives two crossings of opposite sign, so Lk = 0; Lk₀ is the ring’s length; Wr is fixed","each turn gives two crossings of one sign; Lk₀ = N/10.5; Tw and Wr are each whole and each fixed"],"why":"Lk = Tw + Wr is held in trust."},
 {"u":"34·bio.3.1","q":"What does the cut-and-pass technique say, and why does passing a stretch of double strand through another change Lk by 2 and never by 1?","a":"passing one strand through a cut in the other changes Lk by exactly ±1, since one crossing of A with B switches sign; passing a double strand through a cut in both strands of another switches four crossings, of which the two of A with B count, each by ±1 the same way: ±2","w":["one strand through the other changes Lk by ±2; a double strand changes it by ±4","every cut changes Lk by ±1, whichever kind; the double pass switches one crossing","a double strand through another switches four crossings that all count, so ±4, or 0 when they cancel"],"why":"From 390 the second kind reaches 414 or 416 and never 415."},
 {"u":"34·bio.4.1","q":"What does the topoisomer-bell technique say, where does the bell come from, and why are the bands on a gel sharp?","a":"in a tube of relaxed rings the share with linking count Lk goes as e^(−K(Lk − Lk₀)²/N), a bell about Lk₀ with spread N/(2K); it comes from the measured strain energy (KkT/N)(Lk − Lk₀)² and the energy-share technique; each ring’s count is a whole number, exactly, so each band is sharp, and the bell is the heights of the bands","w":["every ring has Lk = Lk₀ exactly; the bell is the blur of the gel; the bands are sharp because Lk₀ is whole","the shares go as e^(−K(Lk − Lk₀)²/N); the bell comes from the rings’ lengths differing; the bands are blurred because counts are not whole","the shares are equal over all counts; there is no bell; the bands are sharp by the enzyme’s cut"],"why":"A crowd can average a count that none of its members can have."},
 {"u":"34·bio.5.1","q":"Why must the parent ring’s strands lose all their turns before the daughters separate, and what does the ring-pass technique say about two linked daughters?","a":"the strands A and B start with Lk about N/10.5 and end in different daughter rings, which lie apart only with Lk = 0, and no moving changes Lk, so cuts must remove it all; turns not removed leave the daughters linked, and each pass of one daughter through the other changes their linking count by exactly ±1","w":["the strands can be pulled apart by moving alone, since Lk changes as the ring jostles; linked daughters separate without cuts","the strands must lose half their turns; each pass of one daughter through the other changes the count by ±2","the strands start with Lk = 0; the daughters are never linked"],"why":"Count 0 is not enough: two rings can hang together with lk = 0."},
 {"u":"34·bio.6.1","q":"Why can no moving untie a ring tied in the overhand knot, and what does one pass of the double strand through itself do to the drawing?","a":"the overhand knot has 9 three-colourings and the plain ring 3, a number of the knot that differs; a pass switches one crossing, the strand that was over there now lying under, and the three-crossing drawing left is a plain ring, undone by a twist and a poke","w":["the overhand knot can be untied by moving, slowly; a pass adds a crossing","the two have the same number of colourings, so the bracket is needed; a pass removes all three crossings at once","no moving unties it because its linking count is 1; a pass changes Lk by 2"],"why":"One pass, and no fewer."},
 {"u":"34·bio.7.1","q":"Why is “coiling a ring tightly changes its linking count” a move to watch out for?","a":"coiling is moving, and no moving changes Lk; it trades twist for coil, and Lk = Tw + Wr stays","w":["coiling changes Lk by 1 for each coil","coiling changes Tw alone and leaves Wr and Lk","coiling cuts a strand"],"why":"Only an enzyme that cuts changes Lk."});
/* end phase F und_r34 */
/* phase F und_r36: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["36·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["36·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"36·bio.1.1","q":"Which light must a pigment remove to look blue, what does the small-gap technique conclude about its energy gap, and which common pigments of living things have gaps too large for that?","a":"The long end, the oranges and reds at 600 nm and beyond; so its gap is at most about 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, the energy of a 600 nm photon; carotenoids, chlorophyll and melanin do not look blue that way","w":["The short end, the blues and violets; so its gap is at least 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; carotenoids are blue pigments","The long end; so its gap is at least about 3.31 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, since longer waves carry more energy; melanin is the common blue pigment","The middle, the greens; so its gap is exactly that of a 500 nm photon; chlorophyll is blue for that reason"],"why":"A longer wavelength is a smaller energy, and small gaps are rare and costly."},
 {"u":"36·bio.2.1","q":"Where does the blue of a Steller’s jay’s feather come from, what does crushing the feather show, and what kind of statement is that test’s result?","a":"From a spongy layer of keratin with evenly spaced air pockets over dark melanin: waves of the blue wavelengths come back in step and add; crushed, the blue goes and grey-brown is left, while a pigment’s red stays; the result is a fact by cases, with a high rate that is not perfect","w":["From a blue pigment in the keratin; crushed, the blue stays; the result is a technique","From air pockets that take away red light as a pigment would; crushed, the feather turns red; the result is a fact by cases with rate 1","From the melanin, which is blue in thin layers; crushed, the blue deepens; the result is a guess"],"why":"Some feathers mix a pigment with a structure."},
 {"u":"36·bio.3.1","q":"What share of light do small things scatter, when does the law fail, how much more is 400 nm scattered than 700 nm, and why does that favour structure over pigment for blue?","a":"A share that goes as 1/λ⁴ while the thing is small against λ; it fails for drops as large as the wavelength, which scatter all colours alike; (700/400)⁴ ≈ 9.4 times; a structure need only be small and fine to send back the short end, while a pigment must find a small gap","w":["A share that goes as 1/λ; it never fails; 1.75 times; a pigment is easier, since it needs no structure","A share that goes as λ⁴; it fails for molecules; 9.4 times as much red as violet; structure is easier because red is common","A share that goes as 1/λ⁴; it fails for molecules of the air; (700/400)² ≈ 3.1 times; neither is easier"],"why":"Large drops scatter every colour alike: clouds are white."},
 {"u":"36·bio.4.1","q":"What makes a small-gap molecule dangerous to its owner, what does the chain-cost technique compute, and what in the worked lines is invented?","a":"Lifted again and again in daylight, it can hand its energy to oxygen, which then attacks the cell; n steps each keeping a share p keep pⁿ, so one unit costs 1/pⁿ of the start; the shares and the step counts are made up to show the shape","w":["It is poisonous in the dark; n steps each keeping p keep n · p; nothing is made up","It hands its energy to oxygen; n steps each keeping p keep pⁿ, so one unit costs pⁿ; the technique itself is made up","It breaks its own bonds with each photon; n steps cost n/p; only the number of steps is made up"],"why":"A cost that grows like a power punishes long chains."},
 {"u":"36·bio.5.1","q":"How does a stack of chitin shelves and air send one wavelength back strongly, what does the tilt-shift technique say, and why does a morpho change colour as it turns while a jay does not?","a":"Waves sent back by one period and the next differ in path, and where the difference is a whole number of wavelengths they add; tilted, the stack sends back a shorter wavelength; the morpho’s layers have one direction, while the jay’s pockets have none and look the same from every side","w":["Each shelf takes away every wavelength but one; tilted, the stack sends back a longer wavelength; the jay’s feather has more melanin","Waves from neighbouring periods add where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths; tilted, the wavelength does not change; the morpho changes because it moves faster","The chitin is a blue pigment in layers; tilted, more layers show; the jay has fewer layers"],"why":"So a morpho flashes blue and darkens toward violet as it turns."});
/* end phase F und_r36 */
