(window.LADDER_CARDS["37·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37·bio.1.1","q":"What does the place-map technique say, what in it is laid down, and where on the strip does 1000 Hz shake it hardest?","a":"Above a few hundred hertz each octave takes about the same length of the strip, about 5 mm of its 35; laid down, as measured on human ears: the best frequency at share x from the far end, f = 165.4(10^(2.1x) − 0.88); 1000 Hz sits at x about 0.40, some 14 mm from the far end","w":["Each hertz takes the same length of the strip; the 35 mm is laid down; 1000 Hz sits at x = 0.05, under 2 mm from the far end","Each octave takes about 5 mm; the formula is proved from the wave equation; 1000 Hz sits halfway, at 17.5 mm","Each octave takes about 5 mm; the formula is laid down; 1000 Hz sits at x about 0.40 from the door, 14 mm from it"],"why":"10^(2.1x) = 1000/165.4 + 0.88, about 6.93."},
 {"u":"37·bio.2.1","q":"What does the place-shift technique say, and why does a step of only two cells suggest the ear uses more than place?","a":"A pitch step of k cents moves the strip’s best place by about 5k/1200 mm; the 5 cents an ear can tell is about 0.022 mm, some two cells, while the peak of the shaking is spread over many cells: too slender a clue alone, so the nerves’ firing in step with the wave, the rate of the repeat, is used too","w":["A step of k cents moves the place by 5k mm; two cells is plenty, so place alone is used","A step of k cents moves the place by about 5k/1200 mm; two cells shows each cell hears one pitch","A step of k cents moves no place at all; the ear uses loudness only"],"why":"That second clue is how a missing fundamental is heard."},
 {"u":"37·bio.3.1","q":"What does the dose technique say, which line of it is laid down, and how long is safe in a nightclub at 103 dB?","a":"If 85 dB is safe for 8 hours, L dB is safe for 8/2^((L − 85)/3) hours: each 3 dB louder halves the time; laid down, from studies of workers: 85 dB for 8 hours a day; 103 dB is six halvings, 8/64 of an hour, seven and a half minutes","w":["Each 10 dB louder halves the time; the 3 dB is laid down; about 2.3 hours","Each 3 dB louder halves the time; 85 dB for 8 hours is laid down; 18/3 = 6 hours","Each 3 dB louder takes an hour off; 85 dB for 8 hours is laid down; 2 hours"],"why":"3 dB is twice the intensity, and the line counts intensity times time."},
 {"u":"37·bio.4.1","q":"What does the returned-shift technique say, which two rules does it join, and how far is a wall whose echo returns 20 ms after the call?","a":"A bat calling at f while flying at v towards a still wall hears the echo at f(c + v)/(c − v); it joins the moving-source rule, c/(c − v), with the moving-listener rule, (c + v)/c; d = 343 × 0.020/2, about 3.4 m","w":["The echo comes back at f c/(c − v), the one-way shift; it uses the moving-source rule alone; about 6.9 m","The echo comes back at f(c + v)/(c − v); it joins the two rules; about 6.9 m, since the sound runs 343 × 0.020","The echo comes back at f, unchanged; no rule is needed; about 3.4 m"],"why":"Distance from the delay, speed from the shift."},
 {"u":"37·bio.5.1","q":"“106 dB is only 6 dB above 100 dB, so it is nearly as safe.” What does the dose technique answer?","a":"6 dB is two halvings: 106 dB is safe for a quarter of the time of 100 dB, under 4 minutes against 15","w":["It is safe for 94% of the time","It is safe for half the time","It is just as safe, since both are above 85 dB"],"why":"Twice the pressure, 6 dB, quarters the time."});
/* end phase F und_r37 */
/* phase F und_r39: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39·bio.1.1","q":"What does the weighted-threshold technique say? Why does either-but-not-both need two layers of units?","a":"With weights 1, 1 a unit is both at threshold 2 and either at threshold 1; with weight −1 and threshold 0 it is not; with weights −1, −1 and threshold −1 it is not-and, so networks of such units build every table (39f1); one unit draws one straight cut through the rows, and no one cut gives either-but-not-both (28·now2), while either and not-and joined by both do","w":["With weights 1, 1 a unit is either at threshold 2 and both at threshold 1; either-but-not-both needs two layers because units have only two inputs","A unit gives 1 when its inputs are equal; one unit gives either-but-not-both with weights 1, −1","Units build only both, either and not; either-but-not-both cannot be built by any network of them"],"why":"A threshold is a stronger gate than a not-and: one unit gives the majority of three."},
 {"u":"39·bio.2.1","q":"What does the rate-ceiling technique say? Why does the rate never reach 1/t_r however strong the drive?","a":"A leaking cell with a rest t_r after each spike fires at rate 1/(t_r + τ ln(RI/(RI − d))) when RI > d, starting from 0 at RI = d and rising with the drive; the climb’s time heads to 0 as the drive grows but is never 0, so the period stays above t_r","w":["The cell fires at rate RI/d, in proportion to the drive; it has no ceiling","The cell fires at rate 1/t_r for every drive above d; the rate reaches the ceiling at once","The cell fires at rate 1/(τ ln(RI/(RI − d))); the ceiling is 1/τ, set by the leak"],"why":"With t_r = 2 ms the rate heads toward 500 a second and stays below it."},
 {"u":"39·bio.3.1","q":"What does the coincidence-window technique say? Why does the window shrink to nothing as d heads to 2k?","a":"A cell with threshold d, kicked by k at time 0 and again at time Δ, with k < d ≤ 2k, fires exactly when Δ ≤ τ ln(k/(d − k)); as d heads to 2k, k/(d − k) heads to 1 and its log to 0: the first kick must not have leaked at all","w":["The cell fires when Δ ≥ τ ln(k/(d − k)); the window shrinks because the kicks grow","The cell fires whenever it gets two kicks, at any Δ; the window is the whole of time","The cell fires when Δ ≤ τ k/d; the window shrinks to nothing as d heads to k"],"why":"With τ = 10 ms, k = 10 mV, d = 15 mV the window is about 6.9 ms."},
 {"u":"39·bio.4.1","q":"What does the inhibitory-ring technique say? Why does a ring of two cells that silence each other hold a state rather than run?","a":"A ring of an odd number n of steadily driven cells, each silenced by the one before, runs as a clock of period n(δ + τ ln(RI/(RI − d))), faster with a stronger drive; two cells are an even ring: a change comes back as it left, so one fires, the other stays silenced, and the pair holds which fired first (39h)","w":["A ring of any number of cells runs as a clock of period n δ; two cells run at twice the speed of four","A ring of an even number n of cells runs as a clock of period n(δ + τ ln(RI/(RI − d))); two cells run, three hold a state","A ring of an odd number of cells holds a state; a ring of two is the fastest clock"],"why":"Three cells with the step’s numbers give about 22 cycles a second."},
 {"u":"39·bio.5.1","q":"Why do two kicks a long way apart not fire the cell? Why is an even ring of silencing cells a memory, not a clock?","a":"The two add only within the window τ ln(k/(d − k)); later, the first has leaked away, and one kick alone is below d; and an even ring holds a state, since only an odd ring brings a change back turned over","w":["Two kicks always add to 2k, which is at least d; and an even ring runs at half the speed of an odd one","The second kick cancels the first; and an even ring has no cells firing at all","The cell is at rest after the first kick for t_r; and an even ring is a clock with a longer period"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r39 */
/* phase F und_r42: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·bio"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·bio"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·bio.1.1","q":"What does the graft technique say? Why does a tree of descent with k ends have 2k − 3 links?","a":"There are 1 · 3 · 5 ⋯ (2n − 5) trees of descent with n named ends, for n ≥ 3, since a new end k + 1 grafts onto the middle of any one of the 2k − 3 links; the inside dots have three links each, so the ends-count technique gives k − 2 inside dots, 2k − 2 dots in all, and a tree has one link fewer than dots","w":["There are n! trees of descent with n ends; a tree with k ends has k − 1 links, since the ends are its only dots","There are 2ⁿ trees of descent; a tree with k ends has 3k links, three at each end","There are 1 · 3 · 5 ⋯ (2n − 5) trees; a tree with k ends has 2k − 3 links because each end has two links"],"why":"T(10) = 2 027 025, and T(20) is about 2.2 × 10²⁰."},
 {"u":"42·bio.2.1","q":"What does the four-point technique say? Why do two of the three sums cross the middle stretch and one does not?","a":"If a table of distances fits a tree, then for every four ends p, q, r, s, of the three sums d(p, q) + d(r, s), d(p, r) + d(q, s), d(p, s) + d(q, r), the two largest are equal; with p, q on one side of a middle stretch of length m, the paths p to q and r to s miss the middle, and each path of the other two pairings crosses it once, adding 2m","w":["Of the three sums the two smallest are equal; the pairing of neighbours crosses the middle twice","All three sums are equal for every tree; no path crosses the middle","The largest sum is the total of the other two; one pairing crosses the middle and two do not"],"why":"The pairing with the smallest sum shows which ends sit together."},
 {"u":"42·bio.3.1","q":"How does the four-point technique show that human and horse sit together? How is the length of the human end link found from three distances?","a":"The sums are 12 + 17 = 29 for human–horse with chicken–tuna, and 32 and 32 for the other two pairings; the smallest sum pairs human with horse, with middle stretch (32 − 29)/2 = 1.5; the human end link is half of human–horse plus human–chicken less horse–chicken: ½(12 + 13 − 11) = 7","w":["Human and horse differ in the fewest places, 11; the human link is half of 12","The sums are 29, 32 and 34, so no tree fits; the human link is the average of its three distances","The largest sum pairs human with horse; the human link is 12 − 5 − 1.5"],"why":"Check: human to tuna is 7 + 1.5 + 12.5 = 21."},
 {"u":"42·bio.4.1","q":"Why does a steady clock put the root at the midpoint of the longest path? How is the rate of one change per 49 million years found, and what does it predict for the human and tuna split?","a":"If chains change at a steady rate on every line of descent, every end is the same distance from the root, so the root is the midpoint of the longest path between two ends; human and chicken differ by 13, so 13/2 changes per line in the 320 million years since mammals and birds split, one per about 49; human–tuna gives 21/2 · 49 ≈ 520 million years, where fossils say about 430","w":["The root is at the end with the fewest changes; the rate comes from human–horse, 12 in 90 million years; it predicts 160 million years","The root is the midpoint because trees are symmetric; the rate is 13 changes per 320 million years on each line; it predicts 260 million years","A steady clock puts the root at the middle stretch; the rate is found from the tuna; it predicts 430 million years exactly"],"why":"For horse and human the clock says 290 million years and fossils about 90."},
 {"u":"42·bio.5.1","q":"Why do seen differences undercount the changes? How does the rare-events technique give d = −ln(1 − p)?","a":"Two chains that differ at a place may have changed there twice, and two that agree may have changed and changed back; with changes striking each place as rare events at an average of d per place, a place is never struck with chance e⁻ᵈ, so the share that differ is p = 1 − e⁻ᵈ, and undoing exp by ln gives d","w":["Differences overcount, since each change shows twice; d = ln(1 + p)","Seen differences are exact; the technique gives d = p","Changes are missed because chains differ in length; d = −ln p"],"why":"Human–tuna: p ≈ 0.202, d ≈ 0.226, about 23.5 changes for 21 seen."},
 {"u":"42·bio.6.1","q":"Why is the tuna not an ancestor of the other three? Why does fitting a tree not show that the clock is steady?","a":"Every end is a living kind; ancestors are inside dots, and none is alive; and any tree with lengths fits its own table: the clock asks more, that the ends be equally far from a root","w":["The tuna is the ancestor, being farthest; and a table that fits a tree has a steady clock","The tuna is too young; and a tree fits only when the clock is steady","Ancestors are the ends with the longest links; and the clock is steady whenever four ends are used"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r42 */
